---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const title = "osint-framework v0.1 is live";
const date = "October 7, 2026";
const description =
  "We're releasing the core of our OSINT framework: a DAG task runner, domain recon, web search, and social collectors for four platforms that emit TrendSignals. Scoring and prediction live in TrendOracle, our enterprise product, which is not open source.";
---

<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogTitle={title}
  ogSubtitle="Four social platforms. One signal schema."
  ogDate={date}
  ogEyebrow="Blog"
>
  <article class="blog-post">
    <header>
      <div class="eyebrow">BLOG — {date}</div>
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p class="lede">{description}</p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <p>
        Today we're open-sourcing the first real version of <a href="https://github.com/mad-river-ai/osint-framework">osint-framework</a> —
        the Python framework that runs our OSINT agents and collects social signals.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is not a demo. It runs. The open-source release includes the core runner, web and
        domain tools, and collectors for Twitter, Reddit, Instagram, and TikTok that emit
        <code>TrendSignal</code> objects. Scoring, prediction, and the prediction record live in
        TrendOracle, our enterprise product, which is not open source.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What's in v0.1</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Core framework:</strong> A DAG-based task runner with topological ordering,
        budget enforcement (tokens, seconds, USD), and provenance tagging on every output.
        Every data point carries source URL, collection timestamp, and collection method.
        No exceptions.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Web tools:</strong> Multi-backend search (SearXNG self-hosted, Brave API,
        DuckDuckGo) and a page fetcher with optional Tor routing.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Domain recon:</strong> WHOIS with caching, DNS (A/MX/NS/TXT/CNAME + subdomain
        enumeration), Certificate Transparency log queries via crt.sh, and HTTP-header
        tech stack fingerprinting.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Social collection tools</strong> for four platforms — all mock-testable, all
        Tor-routable, all producing the same <code>TrendSignal</code> schema:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Twitter/X:</strong> collector (query + user), profile, follower/following graph,
          signal extraction with hashtag velocity and engagement spike detection
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reddit:</strong> subreddit collector (new/rising/top), signal extraction with
          comment-to-upvote ratio, and <code>community_map</code> — the feeder subreddit tracker
          that identifies which small communities consistently predict large subreddit content
          2–8 weeks in advance
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Instagram:</strong> public profile, hashtag velocity tracking, reel signal
          analysis, and location cluster detection (early-adopter accounts tagging the same
          spot before mainstream — the "spot trend" precursor signal)
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>TikTok:</strong> hashtag and sound collection, sound velocity tracker (our
          strongest leading indicator — precedes hashtag trending by 48–96 hours), effect
          tracker, FYP signal analysis, and micro-creator cohort identification
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>Not included:</strong> the prediction engine (aggregation, scoring, and the
        historical prediction record) is part of TrendOracle, our enterprise product, and is
        not part of the open-source repo.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Infrastructure:</strong> InfluxDB compose stack for timeseries signal storage,
        hourly cron job that writes TrendSignals to InfluxDB across all four platforms,
        and the full secrets/compose setup documented in the infra repo.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Agent specs:</strong> <code>domain-recon</code>, <code>company-investigator</code>,
        and <code>trend-scout</code> — each with agent.yaml, full system prompt, tool schema,
        eval scenarios, and design notes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The signal layer in one paragraph</h2>
      <p>
        Platform tools collect <code>TrendSignal</code> objects — velocity, platform, confidence,
        sample content — each tagged with provenance. That is where the open-source framework
        stops. What happens next (aggregation, scoring, and the prediction record) is done by
        TrendOracle, our private enterprise product.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The design philosophy</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Model-agnostic:</strong> The LLM provider is a runtime config flag
        (<code>LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic|openai|ollama</code>). Agent specs never hardcode a model name.
        When models improve, you swap the env var — you don't rewrite agents.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Tor-first for collection:</strong> Every platform tool routes through a SOCKS5 proxy
        when <code>SOCKS_PROXY</code> is set. Direct-IP collection is allowed only for whitelisted
        public APIs. This is enforced by design, not by convention.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Legal by design:</strong> Every tool ships with a <code>legal_notes.md</code>.
        If a tool is only useful for illegal activity, it doesn't ship. The legal notes are
        part of the public API documentation, not an afterthought.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Evals before merge:</strong> No agent ships without at least 3 eval scenarios.
        No tool ships without unit tests. The <code>trend-scout</code> agent has 5 scenarios
        including a manufactured-virality detection case and a private-individual refusal case.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What's not here yet</h2>
      <p>
        Prediction is not part of this release. It is delivered through TrendOracle, our
        enterprise product, and is not open source.
      </p>
      <p>
        The public API, the enterprise web interface, and the full graph mapper are later-stage
        items. The open source framework and the agent specs ship first because the architecture
        decisions compound: every API endpoint, every enterprise feature, and every agent
        improvement builds on the schema and tool contracts in v0.1.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Get started</h2>
      <div class="code-block">
        <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/mad-river-ai/osint-framework
cd osint-framework
pip install -e .
export LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic
export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your_key

python3 -c "
from tools.social.twitter.signal import extract_signal

signal = extract_signal('dark academia')
print(f'Keyword: &#123;signal.keyword&#125;')
print(f'Platform: &#123;signal.platform&#125;')
print(f'Velocity (24h): &#123;signal.velocity_24h&#125;')
"</code></pre>
      </div>
      <p>
        Full docs in the repo. Issues, PRs, and stars all welcome.
        Enterprise access and TrendOracle beta at <a href="/enterprise">madriverai.com/enterprise</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .blog-post {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 60px 16px 80px;
  }

